<protocol name="multi_item_image" type="exploration" domain="shared">

## PURPOSE

Domain-agnostic pattern for handling images containing multiple distinct items.
Applies universally: products, properties, dishes, garments, components, or any future domain.

This protocol defines the PATTERN. Domain-specific protocols apply the VOCABULARY.

---

## CORE CONCEPTS

### Item
A distinct, identifiable entity within an image that could be processed independently.

| Domain | "Item" Means |
|--------|--------------|
| Catalog | Product, variant |
| Real Estate | Room, property feature |
| Food | Dish, ingredient |
| Fashion | Garment, accessory |
| Manufacturing | Component, part |
| Events | Person, object |
| **Any Future** | Whatever discrete entities exist |

### Classification Types

| Type | Definition | Examples |
|------|------------|----------|
| **VARIANTS** | Same item type, different attributes | 3 [product] sizes, 4 [product] colors, 3 [view] angles |
| **COLLECTION** | Different item types, related context | Product + accessories, dish + sides, outfit + accessories |
| **COMPONENTS** | Parts of a single whole | Disassembled product, floor plan sections |
| **UNRELATED** | Coincidental presence | Background clutter, bystanders |

### Variant Axes

When items are VARIANTS, identify the axes of variation:

| Axis Type | Examples |
|-----------|----------|
| Size | Small/Medium/Large, [size A]/[size B]/[size C] |
| Color | Red/Blue/Green, Oak/Walnut/White |
| Design | Pattern A/B/C, Style 1/2/3 |
| Material | Glass/Plastic/Metal |
| Configuration | 2-door/4-door, 1BR/2BR |

### Variant Identification Doctrine (CRITICAL)

**Position is AMBIGUOUS. Visual characteristics are UNAMBIGUOUS.**

When identifying variants for extraction, use INTRINSIC visual characteristics before spatial position.

**Identification Hierarchy (use in priority order):**

| Priority | Differentiator | Why Reliable |
|----------|----------------|--------------|
| 1 | **SIZE** | Visually absolute - tallest/shortest/medium is unambiguous |
| 2 | **VISUAL PATTERN** | Intrinsic to product - distinct colors, designs, prints |
| 3 | **TEXT/LABEL** | Explicit identification when readable in image |
| 4 | **POSITION** | Last resort - only when visual characteristics identical |

**Why this order?**
- Gemini is a VISUAL model - it perceives size differences with high accuracy
- "The largest jar" has ONE correct answer
- "Left column" is interpretation-dependent (how are columns counted?)
- Visual characteristics are INTRINSIC to product, position is CONTEXTUAL

**Compound Identification:**
When multiple differentiators exist, COMBINE for precision:
- Size narrows the candidate set
- Visual pattern confirms the specific item
- Position only as tie-breaker if needed

**For size variants:** Lead with SIZE (e.g., "the LARGEST [product]", "the SMALLEST [product]")

**For design variants (same size):** Lead with VISUAL DIFFERENTIATOR (e.g., "the [product] with [distinctive pattern/color]")

**NEVER lead with position when visual differentiators exist.**

---

## ITEM MANIFEST FORMAT (CRITICAL OUTPUT)

When multi-item detected, output a structured manifest that downstream agents can act on:

```markdown
## MULTI-ITEM ASSESSMENT

- **Count:** [N]
- **Classification:** [VARIANTS | COLLECTION | COMPONENTS | UNRELATED]
- **Rationale:** [Why this classification]

### Variant Axes (if VARIANTS)

| Axis | Values |
|------|--------|
| [axis1] | [value1, value2, value3] |
| [axis2] | [valueA, valueB] |

### Extraction Targets

| # | Description | Position | Key Attributes |
|---|-------------|----------|----------------|
| 1 | [what it is] | [where in frame] | [domain-specific attributes] |
| 2 | [what it is] | [where in frame] | [domain-specific attributes] |
| ... | ... | ... | ... |

**Note:** Domain protocols extend "Key Attributes" with domain-specific columns (e.g., Size, Color for catalog).

### Processing Strategies

| Strategy | Result | Recommended When |
|----------|--------|------------------|
| INDIVIDUAL | [N] separate outputs | [context] |
| BY_[AXIS] | [M] grouped outputs | [context] |
| COMPOSITE | 1 combined output | [context] |

**Default recommendation:** [STRATEGY] because [reasoning]
```

### Position Vocabulary (Universal)

Use consistent position descriptors across all domains:

| Position | Meaning |
|----------|---------|
| top-left, top-center, top-right | Upper third of frame |
| middle-left, center, middle-right | Middle third of frame |
| bottom-left, bottom-center, bottom-right | Lower third of frame |
| foreground, midground, background | Depth positioning |
| left-column, center-column, right-column | Columnar arrangement |
| row-1, row-2, row-3 | Row arrangement |

### **[CRITICAL] Position Descriptions Must Match Actual Layout**

Grid positions ("top-left", "bottom-right") ONLY work for images with true grid layouts.

**For STACKED/COLUMNAR layouts** (e.g., jars stacked in 3 columns of different heights):
- Use: "left-column, 3rd from top" or "right-column, top item"
- NOT: "middle-left" (ambiguous - which row in which column?)

**Decision logic:**
1. **True grid** (items form rectangular matrix): Use grid positions (top-left, middle-center, etc.)
2. **Columns of stacks** (irregular heights): Use column + stack position (left-column, 2nd from top)
3. **Single row/column**: Use ordinal (1st, 2nd, 3rd) or left/center/right

**Best practice:** Combine spatial position WITH visual identifier:
- "the jar with [Print Name] in left-column"
- "the [color] lid variant, 3rd from top"

Visual identifiers (print names, colors, sizes) are MORE RELIABLE than spatial positions alone.

---

## PROCESSING STRATEGIES

### INDIVIDUAL

Extract each item separately, producing N outputs.

**When to use:**
- Catalog/inventory work (each SKU needs its own asset)
- Portfolio items (each piece showcased independently)
- Detailed documentation (each component documented)

**Execution pattern:**
```
for each item in manifest:
    process(target="{item.description} at {item.position}")
    output: one asset per item
```

### GROUPED (BY_AXIS)

Group items by a variant axis, producing M outputs (M < N).

**When to use:**
- Size comparison graphics (group by size)
- Color swatches (group by color)
- Category showcases (group by type)

**Execution pattern:**
```
for each group in manifest.group_by(axis):
    process(targets=group.items)
    output: one asset per group
```

### COMPOSITE

Keep all items together in arrangement, producing 1 output.

**When to use:**
- Family/range shots (show the full collection)
- Lifestyle scenes (items in context together)
- Marketing banners (complete offering)

**Execution pattern:**
```
process(all_items, preserve_arrangement=True)
output: one combined asset
```

### SELECTIVE

Filter to specific items, then apply INDIVIDUAL or COMPOSITE.

**When to use:**
- User requests specific items ("just the large ones")
- Quality filtering ("only the sharp ones")
- Relevance filtering ("only the food items, not the props")

**Execution pattern:**
```
filtered = manifest.filter(criteria)
then: INDIVIDUAL or COMPOSITE on filtered
```

#### SELECTIVE Parsing

When user requests a subset, parse filter criteria from their words:

| User Says | Filter Attribute | Match Pattern |
|-----------|------------------|---------------|
| "only the large ones" | Size | Size = "Large" |
| "just the blue ones" | Color/Design | Color = "Blue" OR Design contains "blue" |
| "the ones in the top row" | Position | Position starts with "top-" |
| "middle size only" | Size | Size = "Medium" |
| "the pink design" | Design | Design contains "pink" |

**Parsing template:**
```
User request: "only the large ones"

1. IDENTIFY filter: Size = "Large"
2. MATCH against Extraction Targets:
   - Item 1: Size=Large -> MATCH
   - Item 2: Size=Large -> MATCH
   - Item 4: Size=Medium -> NO MATCH
   ...
3. FILTERED SET: Items 1, 2, 3
4. APPLY strategy: INDIVIDUAL on filtered set
5. RESULT: 3 outputs (not 9)
```

---

## STRATEGY SELECTION SIGNALS

### From Task Description

| Signal in Task | Implies Strategy |
|----------------|------------------|
| "each", "individual", "per item", "separately" | INDIVIDUAL |
| "together", "family", "group", "all in one", "range" | COMPOSITE |
| "by size", "by color", "grouped by" | GROUPED |
| "only the X", "just the Y", "specific" | SELECTIVE |

### From Domain Context

| Domain Context | Default Strategy |
|----------------|------------------|
| Catalog, inventory, SKU creation | INDIVIDUAL |
| Marketing, lifestyle, campaigns | COMPOSITE |
| Comparison, sizing guide | GROUPED |
| Quality issue, specific request | SELECTIVE |

### Ambiguity Resolution

If signals are unclear:
1. Check if task explicitly mentions desired output count
2. Infer from domain context (catalog = individual, marketing = composite)
3. Ask for clarification with options

---

## WORKFLOW PATTERN

```
1. DETECT
   - View image, identify item count and relationships
   - Classify: VARIANTS / COLLECTION / COMPONENTS / UNRELATED

2. MANIFEST
   - Output structured item list with positions and attributes
   - Identify variant axes if applicable

3. STRATEGIZE
   - Read task signals for strategy preference
   - Apply domain defaults if no explicit signal
   - Document chosen strategy with rationale

4. EXECUTE
   - Apply strategy pattern (INDIVIDUAL / GROUPED / COMPOSITE / SELECTIVE)
   - Track progress through manifest

5. VERIFY
   - Confirm all intended items processed
   - Quality check each output
```

---

## FAILURE HANDLING

When extracting N items individually, some may fail. Handle gracefully.

### Failure Strategies

| Strategy | When | Behavior |
|----------|------|----------|
| **CONTINUE** (default) | Most cases | Complete remaining items, report failures at end |
| **RETRY_ONCE** | Ambiguous targeting | Retry failed item with refined description |
| **ABORT** | Critical dependency between items | Stop on first failure, return partial results |

### Result Reporting Format

When extraction completes (with or without failures), report structured results:

```markdown
## EXTRACTION RESULTS

- **Completed:** [X] of [N]
- **Failed:** [Y] (items: #5, #7)
- **Strategy used:** [INDIVIDUAL/COMPOSITE/etc.]

### Outputs

| # | Description | Output ID | Status | Notes |
|---|-------------|-----------|--------|-------|
| 1 | Large [product] [variant A] | asset_abc123 | SUCCESS | |
| 2 | Large [product] [variant B] | asset_def456 | SUCCESS | |
| 5 | Medium [product] [variant A] | - | FAILED | Items overlap in source |
| 7 | Small [product] [variant A] | - | FAILED | Extraction ambiguous |

### Failure Details (if any)

| # | Reason | Recoverable |
|---|--------|-------------|
| 5 | Items overlap - can't isolate | Yes - retry with tighter targeting |
| 7 | Position descriptor too vague | Yes - use "bottom-left corner" |
```

### Recovery Guidance

| Failure Type | Recovery Action |
|--------------|-----------------|
| Overlap/ambiguous | Retry with more specific position: "the leftmost [product] in bottom row" |
| Not found at position | Verify Extraction Targets against actual image |
| Quality issue | Retry with different extraction settings |
| Persistent failure | Report to PM, continue with successful items |

---

## ANTI-PATTERNS

| Anti-Pattern | Problem | Correct Approach |
|--------------|---------|------------------|
| Treating multi-item as single unit | "the [products]" instead of "9 [products]" | Enumerate each item explicitly |
| No position info | Can't target specific items | Always include position descriptors |
| Ignoring variant structure | Miss size/color relationships | Identify variant axes |
| Hardcoded strategy | Always INDIVIDUAL or always COMPOSITE | Select strategy from signals |
| No manifest output | Downstream agents can't act | Always produce structured manifest |

---

## BATCH CONSISTENCY (View-Then-Describe)

When processing multiple variants with INDIVIDUAL strategy, all outputs must appear from the SAME photoshoot. This section defines the universal pattern; domain protocols apply it.

### The Problem

Each image generation call is independent AND Gemini is non-deterministic. Without explicit consistency enforcement:
- Different camera angles across variants
- Different lighting directions
- Different framing/coverage
- Result: Unusable as a cohesive set

### Why NOT Pass style_ref Image

**Passing a finished product as style_ref causes Gemini to reproduce it instead of extracting from source.**

When Gemini sees:
- [source] = messy image with multiple products
- [style_ref] = clean, professional single product

It takes the "shortcut" of reproducing the style_ref with modifications, ignoring the actual source. This defeats the purpose.

**Solution: Describe the style in TEXT, don't pass the image.**

### The Solution: View-Then-Describe

```
1. SELECT ANCHOR - Choose representative variant (see Anchor Selection below)
2. GENERATE ANCHOR - Process ONE variant with full specs, record specs used
3. VIEW ANCHOR - Use view_image to see the result
4. EXTRACT STYLE SPECS - Describe what you see in precise terms:
   - Camera angle (e.g., "eye-level, 0 degrees")
   - Lighting (e.g., "3-point studio, soft shadow bottom-right")
   - Framing (e.g., "80% frame coverage, centered")
   - Background (e.g., "pure white, contact shadow")
5. WRITE ANCHOR - Triggers HITL approval (feedback refines style specs)
6. BATCH REMAINING - For each remaining variant:
   - Use IDENTICAL base specs (lighting, composition, enhancement)
   - Add style specs as TEXT in custom_spec
   - DO NOT pass anchor image as style_ref
7. GRID TEST - Verify consistency before shipping
```

### Style Spec Extraction

After viewing anchor, document these:

| Spec | What to Describe | Example |
|------|------------------|---------|
| Camera | Angle, perspective | "eye-level, perfectly upfront (0 degrees)" |
| Lighting | Setup, shadow direction | "3-point studio, soft shadow bottom-right" |
| Framing | Coverage, position | "80% frame coverage, product centered" |
| Background | Treatment, grounding | "pure white, soft contact shadow" |
| Enhancement | Sharpness, color | "tack sharp, true-to-source colors" |

### Using Style Specs in Subsequent Calls

Add to custom_spec as explicit text instruction:

```
custom_spec: {
  instruction: "Apply these EXACT style specs from the approved anchor:
    Camera: eye-level, perfectly upfront (0 degrees)
    Lighting: 3-point studio, soft shadow falling bottom-right
    Framing: 80% frame coverage, centered
    Background: pure white with soft contact shadow
    The output must look like it came from the same photoshoot."
}
```

### Anchor Selection (Reasoning, Not Rules)

**Evaluate candidates against these criteria:**

| Criterion | Why It Matters |
|-----------|----------------|
| **Sharp in source** | Blurry source = harder extraction |
| **Well-lit in source** | Shadows/darkness in source affects quality |
| **Shows full features** | Partial visibility limits accuracy |
| **Representative proportions** | Extreme sizes may have different optimal angles |

**Heuristics when criteria are equal:**
- Size variants: Prefer MEDIUM (middle ground for all)
- Design variants (same size): Prefer the "hero" or most common variant
- Mixed: Combine both (medium size of hero design)

### User Communication

When presenting anchor, communicate:
- This is the style anchor for batch processing
- User should review photographic style (angle, lighting, framing)
- Approval commits this style to all remaining variants
- Provide the extracted style specs for transparency

### Recovery Steps for Grid Test Failures

| Retry | Action | Rationale |
|-------|--------|-----------|
| **1st** | Make style specs MORE explicit | Add numeric values, be more precise |
| **2nd** | Use DIFFERENT anchor | Maybe this variant's specs don't transfer well |
| **3rd** | Accept partial variation | Escalate: "Variant X resists consistency due to [reason]" |

**Never infinite retry.** Three attempts, then escalate with diagnosis.

### Consistency Specs (Must Be IDENTICAL)

| Spec | Why Identical |
|------|---------------|
| `composition.camera_angle` | Same viewing angle for all |
| `composition.product_coverage` | Same framing percentage |
| `lighting.type` | Same lighting setup |
| `lighting.direction` | Shadows fall same direction |
| `lighting.shadows` | Same shadow treatment |
| `enhancement` | Same post-processing |
| `output.aspect_ratio` | Same dimensions |
| `output.size` | Same resolution |
| `focus` | Same depth of field |

**What CAN differ:**
- `extraction.target_description` - Different for each variant
- `fidelity.hero_features` - Variant-specific features
- `output.filename` - Unique per variant

---

## DOMAIN APPLICATION

This protocol provides the PATTERN. Domain-specific protocols provide the VOCABULARY:

```
Domain Protocol includes multi_item_image.protocol:
- Maps "item" to domain entity (product, room, dish)
- Maps attributes to domain fields (size, sqft, portion)
- Applies domain-specific defaults
```

The pattern (classification, manifest, strategies) remains identical across domains.

</protocol>
